-- Q1: hard / L3 — Partnership dissociation under RUPA (dissociation vs dissolution)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Harmon & Blake is a two-person general partnership. Harmon notifies Blake on April 1, 2026, that she is withdrawing from the partnership effective immediately. Under RUPA, which statement best describes the legal consequences of Harmon''s dissociation?', '["The partnership is not automatically dissolved; Blake may continue the business by buying out Harmon''s interest at fair value", "The partnership is immediately dissolved and must wind up its affairs within 90 days", "Harmon retains liability for all future partnership obligations until formal dissolution is filed with the state", "Harmon''s withdrawal is void because a partner cannot unilaterally dissociate from a partnership"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under RUPA Section 601, a partner has the power to dissociate at any time by express will. Section 701 requires the partnership to buy out the dissociated partner''s interest at fair value. Unlike the UPA, RUPA distinguishes dissociation from dissolution — a partnership may continue after a partner leaves. Choice B incorrectly conflates dissociation with automatic dissolution under the older UPA framework.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — C corporation double taxation calculation
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Pinnacle Corp., a C corporation, earns $200,000 of taxable income in 2026. It distributes all after-tax income as qualified dividends to its sole shareholder, who is in the 15% dividend tax bracket. What is the total combined tax burden on this income?', '["$42,000", "$65,700", "$72,000", "$30,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 11(b), a C corporation pays a flat 21% tax on taxable income ($200,000 x 21% = $42,000). After-tax income of $158,000 distributed as qualified dividends is taxed at 15% under IRC Section 1(h) ($158,000 x 15% = $23,700), yielding a combined burden of $65,700. This illustrates double taxation. Choice A reflects only the corporate-level tax and ignores the shareholder dividend tax.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Check-the-box election (Form 8832) for LLC classification
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Raven Consulting LLC is a multi-member LLC that wants to be taxed as a corporation rather than as a partnership. Under IRS classification rules, which form must the LLC file to change its default tax treatment?', '["Form 1065", "Form 2553", "Form 8832", "Form 1120"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Treasury Regulation Section 301.7701-3, the check-the-box rules allow eligible entities to elect their tax classification by filing Form 8832 (Entity Classification Election). A multi-member LLC defaults to partnership taxation but may elect C corporation treatment via Form 8832. Choice B (Form 2553) is used specifically for S corporation election, not for the initial entity classification change.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Limited partnership certificate filing requirement
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Weber and Cortez plan to form a limited partnership in which Weber will serve as the general partner and Cortez will be the limited partner. Under the Revised Uniform Limited Partnership Act, which step is required to create the limited partnership?', '["Execute a written partnership agreement signed by both partners", "Register a fictitious business name with the county clerk", "Obtain a federal employer identification number from the IRS", "File a certificate of limited partnership with the state"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the Revised Uniform Limited Partnership Act (RULPA) Section 201, a limited partnership is formed by filing a certificate of limited partnership with the state. This filing requirement distinguishes limited partnerships from general partnerships, which can be formed informally. Choice A describes a best practice but is not legally required to create the entity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Sole proprietorship no continuity of life
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Which characteristic is unique to a sole proprietorship?', '["The business ceases to exist upon the owner''s death", "The owner must file articles of organization with the state", "The owner''s liability is limited to the amount invested", "Business income is reported on Form 1065"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A sole proprietorship has no continuity of life — the business is not a separate legal entity, so it terminates when the owner dies. This distinguishes it from corporations and LLCs, which have perpetual existence. Choice C is incorrect because a sole proprietor has unlimited personal liability for all business obligations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — S corporation eligibility termination (ineligible shareholder)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Sterling Inc. has been a valid S corporation since 2020 with 40 individual shareholders. On March 15, 2026, one shareholder transfers her shares to Apex Partners LP, a domestic limited partnership, and another shareholder sells shares to a nonresident alien. Evaluate the effect on Sterling''s S corporation status.', '["The S election terminates because both a partnership and a nonresident alien are ineligible S corporation shareholders", "The S election terminates only because of the nonresident alien; the domestic partnership is a permitted shareholder", "The S election remains valid because both transfers require board approval before affecting shareholder eligibility", "The S election terminates but only prospectively from the next tax year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1), S corporation shareholders must be individuals, estates, certain trusts, or tax-exempt organizations. Both partnerships (including domestic LPs) and nonresident aliens are ineligible shareholders. Under IRC Section 1362(d)(2), the S election terminates on the date the corporation ceases to meet the requirements. Choice B incorrectly assumes domestic partnerships are eligible — no partnership of any type qualifies.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Automatic stay in bankruptcy
(3, 'Business Structures', 'What is the immediate legal effect when a debtor files a bankruptcy petition?', '["All debts are automatically discharged", "An automatic stay halts most collection actions against the debtor", "The debtor''s assets are immediately liquidated", "Secured creditors lose their liens on collateral"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 362, the filing of a bankruptcy petition triggers an automatic stay that immediately halts most collection efforts, lawsuits, foreclosures, and garnishments against the debtor. The stay provides breathing room while the case is administered. Choice A is incorrect because discharge occurs only after the bankruptcy process is completed, not automatically upon filing.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Default RUPA profit-sharing (equal split regardless of capital)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Feldman contributes $300,000 and Nash contributes $100,000 to form a general partnership. They do not execute a written partnership agreement. At year-end, the partnership earns $200,000 in profits. Under RUPA default rules, how are the profits allocated?', '["$150,000 to Feldman and $50,000 to Nash, based on capital contributions", "$160,000 to Feldman and $40,000 to Nash, proportional to investment", "$100,000 to Feldman and $100,000 to Nash", "$120,000 to Feldman and $80,000 to Nash, based on a weighted formula"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under RUPA Section 401(b), profits are shared equally among partners unless the partnership agreement provides otherwise. Despite unequal capital contributions, the default rule allocates profits 50/50, giving each partner $100,000. Choice A incorrectly applies a capital-contribution ratio — the equal-sharing default is frequently tested because candidates often assume profits follow contributions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Partnership fiduciary duty of loyalty (competing with partnership)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Garrett is a partner in a three-person accounting firm organized as a general partnership. Without informing the other partners, Garrett begins providing tax preparation services to clients on the side, competing directly with the partnership''s business. Under RUPA, which fiduciary duty has Garrett violated?', '["The duty of care", "The duty of good faith and fair dealing", "The duty of obedience", "The duty of loyalty"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under RUPA Section 404(b), the duty of loyalty requires a partner to refrain from competing with the partnership and from dealing with the partnership on behalf of a party with an adverse interest. Garrett''s side business directly competing with the firm violates this duty. Choice A (duty of care) addresses negligent or reckless conduct in partnership activities, not self-interested competition.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Corporation organizational costs deduction with phase-out (IRC §248)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Beacon Enterprises Inc. is formed on January 1, 2026, and incurs $52,000 in organizational expenditures including legal fees, state incorporation fees, and organizational meeting costs. Under IRC Section 248, what is the maximum amount Beacon may immediately deduct in its first tax year?', '["$3,000, because the $5,000 deduction is reduced by the $2,000 exceeding the $50,000 threshold", "$5,000, regardless of total organizational expenditures", "$52,000, because all organizational costs are fully deductible in the first year", "$0, because organizational costs must be capitalized and never deducted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 248, a corporation may deduct up to $5,000 of organizational expenditures immediately, but this deduction is reduced dollar-for-dollar by the amount exceeding $50,000. Beacon''s $52,000 exceeds the threshold by $2,000, reducing the immediate deduction to $3,000. The remaining $49,000 is amortized over 180 months. Choice B ignores the phase-out reduction that applies when organizational expenditures exceed $50,000.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — LLC operating agreement as governing document
(3, 'Business Structures', 'What is the primary governing document of a limited liability company?', '["Articles of incorporation", "Operating agreement", "Certificate of limited partnership", "Corporate bylaws"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The operating agreement is the governing document of an LLC, specifying member rights, management structure, profit-sharing arrangements, and transfer of interests. State LLC statutes recognize this document as controlling internal governance. Choice A (articles of incorporation) and choice D (corporate bylaws) are governance documents for corporations, not LLCs.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Involuntary bankruptcy petition (three-creditor rule)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'A group of unsecured creditors seeks to file an involuntary bankruptcy petition against Dalton Manufacturing Co. under Chapter 7. Dalton has 15 creditors. Under federal bankruptcy law, what is the minimum number of petitioning creditors required to file?', '["One creditor holding a claim of at least $18,600", "Three or more creditors whose aggregate unsecured claims total at least $18,600", "A majority of all creditors by dollar amount", "Any single creditor regardless of the claim amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 303(b), if the debtor has 12 or more creditors, an involuntary petition must be filed by three or more creditors whose aggregate unsecured noncontingent claims total at least the statutory minimum. Since Dalton has 15 creditors, the three-creditor rule applies. Choice A would apply only if the debtor had fewer than 12 creditors, in which case a single creditor could file.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Means test for Chapter 7 eligibility
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Rivera earns $95,000 annually and lives in a state where the median income for her household size is $72,000. She files a Chapter 7 bankruptcy petition and the bankruptcy trustee challenges her eligibility. Under the Bankruptcy Abuse Prevention Act, what is the most likely outcome?', '["Rivera''s petition is automatically approved because Chapter 7 is available to all individuals", "Rivera''s petition is denied because she has assets exceeding the exemption limits", "Rivera''s petition may be dismissed or converted because her income exceeds the state median, triggering the means test", "Rivera''s petition is approved but she must repay 50% of her debts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 707(b), the means test was added by the Bankruptcy Abuse Prevention and Consumer Protection Act of 2005. When a debtor''s current monthly income exceeds the state median for the applicable household size, a presumption of abuse arises, and the case may be dismissed or converted to Chapter 13. Choice A is incorrect because the means test specifically limits Chapter 7 access for higher-income debtors.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Limited partner losing protection by participating in management
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Townsend is a limited partner in Ridgeline Capital LP. Over the past year, Townsend has been negotiating contracts with vendors, hiring employees, and making daily operational decisions for the partnership. A creditor sues for $600,000 and seeks to hold Townsend personally liable. Under RULPA, what is the most likely outcome?', '["Townsend is protected because limited partners can never be held personally liable", "Townsend is protected because only general partners manage the business", "Townsend may be held personally liable because participating in management control can eliminate limited liability protection", "Townsend is liable only up to the amount of her original capital contribution"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under RULPA Section 303, a limited partner who participates in the control of the business may be held personally liable to creditors who reasonably believe the limited partner is a general partner. Townsend''s active role in negotiations, hiring, and daily operations constitutes management control. Choice A is incorrect because limited liability is conditional on not participating in management — the protection is forfeited when this boundary is crossed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Subrogation rights of surety after payment
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Marshall acts as surety for a $250,000 loan from First Union Bank to Pine Creek LLC. When Pine Creek defaults, Marshall pays the full $250,000 to the bank. Under suretyship law, which right allows Marshall to pursue Pine Creek using the bank''s original remedies?', '["Subrogation", "Contribution", "Exoneration", "Indemnification"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under suretyship law, subrogation allows a surety who pays the creditor''s claim to step into the creditor''s shoes and assert all of the creditor''s rights and remedies against the principal debtor. After paying $250,000, Marshall may pursue Pine Creek using the bank''s original security interests and legal claims. Choice B (contribution) applies among co-sureties sharing a joint obligation, not between a surety and the principal debtor.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — S corporation one class of stock requirement
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Which ownership restriction applies specifically to S corporations under the IRC?', '["The corporation must have at least two shareholders", "Annual revenue cannot exceed $10 million", "The corporation cannot operate in more than one state", "The corporation may have only one class of stock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(D), an S corporation may have only one class of stock, although differences in voting rights among shares are permitted. This single-class requirement ensures all shareholders receive identical distribution and liquidation rights. Choice A is incorrect because an S corporation may have as few as one shareholder — there is no minimum shareholder requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Single-member LLC disregarded entity default
(3, 'Business Structures', 'How is a single-member LLC taxed by default under IRS rules?', '["As a C corporation filing Form 1120", "As a disregarded entity with income reported on the owner''s Schedule C", "As a partnership filing Form 1065", "As an S corporation filing Form 1120-S"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Treasury Regulation Section 301.7701-3, a single-member LLC is classified as a disregarded entity by default, meaning the IRS treats it as a sole proprietorship. The owner reports business income and expenses on Schedule C of Form 1040. Choice C is incorrect because partnership treatment is the default for multi-member LLCs, not single-member LLCs.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Chapter 11 cramdown provision
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Oakmont Industries files Chapter 11 bankruptcy and proposes a reorganization plan. The unsecured creditors'' committee votes to reject the plan. The bankruptcy court confirms the plan over the committee''s objection. Under bankruptcy law, this judicial action is known as:', '["Automatic stay extension", "Preference avoidance", "Cramdown", "Discharge injunction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 1129(b), the bankruptcy court may confirm a reorganization plan over the objection of a dissenting class of creditors through cramdown, provided the plan does not discriminate unfairly and is fair and equitable to the dissenting class. This allows viable businesses to reorganize even without unanimous creditor support. Choice B (preference avoidance) refers to the trustee''s power to recover pre-petition preferential transfers, unrelated to plan confirmation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Corporate dissolution definition
(3, 'Business Structures', 'What is the legal process of ending a corporation''s existence called?', '["Dissociation", "Liquidation", "Reorganization", "Dissolution"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dissolution is the legal process of terminating a corporation''s existence, typically initiated by a vote of the board of directors and shareholders or by court order. After dissolution, the corporation winds up its affairs — settling debts, distributing remaining assets, and filing articles of dissolution. Choice A (dissociation) refers to a partner leaving a partnership under RUPA, not the termination of a corporation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — DBA/fictitious name registration for sole proprietors
(3, 'Business Structures', 'What filing may a sole proprietor need when operating under a name other than the owner''s legal name?', '["A fictitious business name (DBA) registration", "Articles of organization", "A certificate of limited partnership", "Corporate bylaws"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When a sole proprietor operates under a trade name different from their own legal name, most states require filing a fictitious business name statement, also called a doing business as (DBA) registration, with the county or state. This filing provides public notice of the owner''s identity. Choice B (articles of organization) is the formation document for an LLC, not a name registration for a sole proprietorship.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Co-surety contribution calculation (proportionate shares)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Drake, Ellis, and Flynn are co-sureties for a $90,000 debt owed by Zenith Corp. Each co-surety has a maximum liability of $60,000. Zenith defaults on the full $90,000, and the creditor collects the entire $60,000 from Drake alone. Evaluate Drake''s right to contribution from the other co-sureties and determine the recovery amounts.', '["Drake may recover $30,000 from Ellis only, because Flynn''s share has been satisfied", "Drake may not recover anything because each co-surety is independently liable to the creditor", "Drake may recover $10,000 from Ellis and $20,000 from Flynn", "Drake may recover $15,000 from Ellis and $15,000 from Flynn"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under suretyship law, co-sureties share the obligation proportionally based on maximum liability. Each surety''s proportionate share is $30,000 ($90,000 x $60,000/$180,000). Drake paid $60,000 but owes only $30,000, creating a $30,000 overpayment. The right of contribution allows Drake to recover $15,000 from each equally situated co-surety. Choice B is incorrect because contribution specifically exists to prevent one co-surety from bearing a disproportionate share.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Preferential transfers in bankruptcy (90-day lookback)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Hartwell Inc. files a Chapter 7 bankruptcy petition on June 1, 2026. On April 15, 2026, Hartwell paid $80,000 to one of its unsecured trade creditors for a pre-existing debt while other similarly situated creditors received nothing. Under bankruptcy law, what is the likely treatment of this payment?', '["The payment is valid because Hartwell had the right to pay any creditor before filing", "The trustee may avoid the payment as a preferential transfer and recover the $80,000 for the estate", "The payment is valid because it occurred more than 30 days before the petition date", "The creditor must return only the amount exceeding fair market value of goods received"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 547(b), a trustee may avoid transfers made within 90 days before filing that enabled the creditor to receive more than it would in a Chapter 7 liquidation. The $80,000 payment 47 days before filing to one creditor while others received nothing is a classic preferential transfer. Choice C is incorrect because the lookback period is 90 days, not 30 days, and 47 days falls well within that window.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Fraudulent conveyance in bankruptcy (constructive fraud)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Langley Corp. is insolvent and anticipates filing bankruptcy. On January 10, 2026, Langley''s CEO transfers the company''s $500,000 commercial property to his spouse for $1. Langley files Chapter 7 on March 15, 2026. The bankruptcy trustee seeks to recover the property. Evaluate the strength of the trustee''s claim under federal bankruptcy law.', '["The trustee cannot recover because transfers between family members are protected under the homestead exemption", "The trustee can recover only if the transfer occurred within 30 days of filing", "The trustee will likely prevail because the transfer for nominal consideration while insolvent is a fraudulent conveyance", "The trustee cannot recover because the property was transferred before the bankruptcy petition was filed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 548, the trustee may avoid fraudulent transfers made within two years before filing. A transfer made while insolvent for less than reasonably equivalent value ($1 for a $500,000 property) constitutes constructive fraud — the trustee need not prove actual intent. Choice D is incorrect because Section 548 specifically grants the trustee power to look back up to two years before the filing date.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Entity choice evaluation (S corp optimal for SE tax reduction)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Kowalski, a solo practitioner, wants to form a new consulting business. She expects $400,000 in net income, wants to minimize self-employment taxes, desires personal liability protection, and plans no outside investors. Evaluate the available entity types and identify the structure that best meets all four objectives simultaneously.', '["Sole proprietorship, because it requires no formation costs and provides full income flexibility", "General partnership, because pass-through taxation eliminates double taxation and allows profit-sharing", "C corporation, because the flat 21% tax rate is lower than individual rates at this income level", "S corporation, because it provides limited liability, avoids double taxation, and allows salary/distribution splitting to reduce self-employment tax"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Sections 1361-1379, an S corporation provides pass-through taxation (avoiding double taxation), limited liability protection, and the ability to split income between reasonable salary (subject to FICA) and distributions (not subject to self-employment tax). At $400,000, this splitting significantly reduces employment taxes. Choice A fails because a sole proprietorship exposes all net income to self-employment tax and provides no liability protection.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Exempt property in Chapter 7 bankruptcy (401(k) vs homestead)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Prescott, an individual debtor, files Chapter 7 bankruptcy in a state allowing federal exemptions. He owns a home with $150,000 in equity, a $45,000 ERISA-qualified 401(k), and $8,000 in household furnishings. The federal homestead exemption is $27,900. Evaluate which assets are most likely protected from liquidation.', '["The 401(k) is fully exempt and the furnishings are exempt up to the applicable limit, but the home equity exceeds the federal homestead exemption", "All three assets are fully exempt because Chapter 7 debtors retain all personal property", "Only the home is exempt because real property receives priority over personal property in bankruptcy", "None of the assets are exempt because total asset value exceeds $100,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 522 and Patterson v. Shumate, ERISA-qualified retirement accounts including 401(k) plans are fully exempt from the bankruptcy estate without dollar limitation. Household furnishings are exempt up to statutory limits. However, $150,000 in home equity far exceeds the $27,900 federal homestead exemption, exposing the excess to liquidation. Choice B is incorrect because Chapter 7 does not protect all property — only assets within exemption limits are retained.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Reaffirmation agreements in Chapter 7 bankruptcy
(3, 'Business Structures', 'In a Chapter 7 bankruptcy, what is a reaffirmation agreement?', '["A court order requiring creditors to stop all collection activities", "A plan for repaying debts over three to five years", "A voluntary agreement by the debtor to remain liable for a specific debt despite the discharge", "A document listing all debts that will be discharged"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under 11 U.S.C. Section 524(c), a reaffirmation agreement is a voluntary contract in which a Chapter 7 debtor agrees to remain personally liable for a particular debt that would otherwise be discharged. Debtors often reaffirm secured debts such as car loans to retain the collateral. Choice B describes a Chapter 13 repayment plan, not a reaffirmation agreement used in Chapter 7 proceedings.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Shareholder derivative lawsuits
(3, 'Business Structures', 'The board of directors of Nexus Corp. refuses to take legal action against its CEO for self-dealing transactions that harmed the corporation. Shareholder Patel brings a lawsuit on behalf of the corporation to recover damages. Under corporate law, this type of legal action is classified as a:', '["Class action lawsuit", "Derivative lawsuit", "Direct action lawsuit", "Interpleader action"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under corporate governance law, a derivative lawsuit is brought by a shareholder on behalf of the corporation when the board refuses to act. Any recovery goes to the corporation, not the individual shareholder. The shareholder must typically first make a demand on the board before filing. Choice C (direct action) would apply only if the shareholder suffered a personal injury distinct from the harm to the corporation as a whole.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — S corporation family shareholder counting rule
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Redwood Inc. is an S corporation with 95 unrelated individual shareholders. The Henderson family — a married couple, their three adult children, and the children''s two spouses — want to invest. Under S corporation shareholder counting rules, how many of the 100-shareholder limit will the Henderson family consume?', '["One, because all members of a family within six generations are treated as a single shareholder", "Two, because only the married couple counts as one and the remaining five count separately", "Seven, because each individual investor is a separate shareholder", "Three, because married couples count as one and the three children count as three"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(1), members of a family (defined as a common ancestor and all lineal descendants within six generations, plus their spouses) are treated as a single shareholder for the 100-shareholder limit. The Henderson family — parents, children, and children''s spouses — all fall within this definition. Choice C incorrectly counts each individual separately, ignoring the family attribution rule designed to facilitate family-owned S corporations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Partnership equal management rights (two-person deadlock)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Ortega and Chen form a general partnership to operate a restaurant. The partnership agreement is silent on management authority. Ortega wants to hire a new head chef, but Chen objects. Under RUPA default rules, how is this ordinary business dispute resolved?', '["Ortega may hire the chef because any partner can act unilaterally on business matters", "Neither partner can act because the partnership must be dissolved to resolve the dispute", "The decision requires a majority vote, and neither partner prevails in a two-person deadlock", "Ortega prevails because the partner who proposed the action first has priority"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under RUPA Section 401(j), differences in ordinary course matters are decided by a majority of the partners. In a two-person partnership, a majority requires both partners to agree. Since Chen objects, there is no majority, and Ortega cannot unilaterally hire the chef. Choice A is incorrect because ordinary business decisions require majority approval, not unilateral action, even though each partner has equal management rights.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L2 — Shareholder limited liability (maximum loss = investment)
(3, 'Business Structures', 'Williams owns 500 shares of stock in GrantTech Inc., a C corporation, for which she paid $25 per share. GrantTech files for bankruptcy and cannot pay its $2 million in outstanding debts. Under corporate law, what is Williams''s maximum personal financial loss?', '["$2,000,000, because shareholders are jointly liable for corporate debts", "$12,500, the amount she paid for her shares", "$40,000, her proportionate share of the corporate debt", "$0, because shareholders have no connection to corporate obligations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under corporate law, shareholders enjoy limited liability — they can lose only the amount invested in their shares. Williams paid $25 x 500 = $12,500, and that is the maximum she can lose. Corporate creditors cannot reach shareholders'' personal assets absent veil piercing. Choice A incorrectly applies joint and several liability, which is a partnership concept — shareholders are not personally liable for corporate debts.', 'easy', 2)
;

